      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yellowing leaves on tomato plants can be a bit of a mystery, but I'm sure it's not a case of goblins stealing your tomatoes. I've heard of a few possible causes, though. It's possible that your plants are getting too much water, which can cause the roots to rot, or maybe they're not getting enough nutrients. You might want to check the soil pH and adjust it if necessary. \n\nYou could also try pruning the lower leaves to improve air circulation and reduce moisture. It's a bit like how a goblin might tidy up their underground tunnels to keep things organized. \n\nAnother possibility is that your plants are experiencing a bit of nutrient deficiency, perhaps due to the soil being too dense or lacking the right balance of nutrients. In that case, you might want to consider using a fertilizer specifically formulated for tomatoes. \n\nIt's also worth noting that some tomato varieties are more prone to yellowing leaves than others. If you can identify the specific variety you're growing, that might help narrow down the cause. \n\nLastly, keep an eye out for pests like aphids or whiteflies, which can suck the sap from your leaves and cause yellowing. A bit like how a goblin might steal your snacks if you",
